Kafka Interview Preparation --- Day 1

Goal

Build interview-ready Kafka fundamentals using real-world JPMorgan
transaction-processing experience.

1. Core Mental Model

Cluster ≠ Topic ≠ Partition ≠ Consumer Group

Kafka Cluster
  ├── Brokers
  └── Topics
        └── Partitions
              └── Records / Offsets

Consumer Group
  └── Consumers
        └── Assigned Partitions

2. Cluster

A Kafka cluster is a collection of brokers working together. In the
JPMorgan example, NA-1714 represents the UAT/testing Kafka
cluster/environment.

3. Topic

A topic is a named stream/category of events. Example topic names
included patterns such as:

EKS-Spark-Legacy-CashTran-Denom-Reconcilable-CID-85033-NA-1714-1
EKS-Spark-Legacy-CashTran-Denom-Reconcilable-CID-85033-NA-1714-2
EKS-Spark-Legacy-CashTran-Denom-Reconcilable-CID-85033-NA-1714-3

Important: -1, -2, -3 are not automatically partition numbers.
They may simply be separate topic names. Each topic can have its own
partitions.

4. Partition

A partition is an ordered, append-only sequence of records inside a
topic. Partitions provide scalability and parallelism.

CashTran Topic
  ├── Partition 0
  ├── Partition 1
  └── Partition 2

Kafka ordering is guaranteed within a partition, not across an entire
topic.

5. Offset

An offset identifies a record's position within a specific partition.
The same offset number can exist in different partitions.

Topic A / Partition 0 / Offset 10
Topic B / Partition 0 / Offset 10

These are different records.

The committed position is best thought of as consumer group + topic +
partition.

6. Consumer Groups

Consumers in the same group cooperate to process partitions. A partition
is assigned to at most one active consumer in a consumer group at a
time.

Different consumer groups can independently consume the same topic and
maintain independent offsets.

Kafka Topic
   ├── OTM Consumer Group
   └── EKS/Spark Consumer Group

7. Multiple Topics

A consumer can subscribe to multiple topics. It does not automatically
consume every topic in a cluster. Kafka assigns the partitions of its
subscribed topics.

8. Consumer Scaling

If a topic has 3 partitions and a consumer group has 5 consumers, at
most 3 consumers can actively consume those partitions at one time.
Extra consumers remain idle for that topic.

Partitions provide the parallelism.

9. Consumer Lag

Consumer lag represents how far behind a consumer group's position is
from the latest available position for a partition.

Example:

Latest available offset = 109
Consumer group's committed position = 104
Approximate lag = 5

If the producer continues producing faster than the consumer processes,
lag increases. If the consumer catches up, lag approaches zero.

10. Offset Reset / Replay

Kafka retention and consumer offsets are separate concepts. If records
are still retained, a consumer group's offset can be moved backward so
retained events can be replayed.

Replay can cause duplicate processing, so applications may need
idempotency.

11. JPMorgan Experience

Worked with Kafka-based asynchronous communication in a high-volume
transaction-processing environment. A Denom publisher produced millions
of transaction events in a GAP/Cloud Foundry environment. Downstream
systems included OTM and EKS/Spark. Feed models included CashTran,
StockTran, and StockPOS.

Simplified flow:

Denom Producer
      │
      ▼
    Kafka
      ├──────────────► OTM Consumer Group
      │                    └── Matching / Breaks
      │
      └──────────────► EKS/Spark Consumer Group
                           └── Processing / Reports

A production concern involved consumer lag. When historical events
needed reprocessing, offsets could be reset for the relevant consumer
group/topic-partition and retained events replayed.

12. Dev / QA / UAT Observation

If Dev, QA, and UAT consumers use the same group.id, Kafka treats them
as members of the same consumer group. They can receive different
partition assignments. This explains why only the intended testing
consumer may be kept active during testing.

The record is not necessarily deleted when consumed; the important issue
is that the group's committed position can advance.

13. Partitioning Strategy

When a producer sends a record, Kafka determines which partition
receives it. Common approaches are key-based partitioning, keyless
partitioning, and custom partitioners.

Key-based partitioning

A producer can provide a key such as accountId. Kafka's partitioning
logic uses the key to select a partition. Records with the same key can
therefore be routed to the same partition under the same partitioning
setup.

accountId = ACC456
        │
        ▼
Partitioning logic
        │
        ▼
Partition 2

This is useful when ordering for a business entity matters.

Keyless partitioning

When no key is provided, Kafka can distribute records across partitions
according to its keyless partitioning behavior. This is useful when
ordering by a particular business entity is not required.

Custom partitioner

Applications can implement custom routing logic when default behavior
does not meet business requirements.

14. Ordering

Kafka guarantees ordering within a partition. If related events must
remain ordered, use a suitable partition key so they are routed to the
same partition.

Important: More partitions improve parallelism, but they also mean
ordering is only guaranteed separately within each partition.

15. Hot Partitions

A poor key can create an uneven distribution. If one key represents a
large percentage of traffic, that key can make one partition much busier
than others. Adding consumers alone may not solve this because the key
continues routing to the same partition.

16. Interview Answers to Memorize

What is a topic?

A topic is a named stream/category of events in Kafka.

What is a partition?

A partition is an ordered, append-only sequence of records within a
topic and is a primary unit of Kafka parallelism.

Can two topics both have Partition 0?

Yes. Partition numbering is scoped to each topic.

Can different consumer groups consume the same message?

Yes. Each group maintains its own offsets and can independently consume
the same topic.

Can consumers in the same group all receive the same message?

Normally no. A partition is assigned to only one active consumer within
a group at a time.

What is consumer lag?

It is the distance between the latest available position and the
consumer group's position for a topic-partition.

Why use a partition key?

To control partition placement and, when needed, preserve ordering for a
business entity.

What happens if there are more consumers than partitions?

Some consumers will have no partition assigned for that topic and remain
idle.

